  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 330 AM EDT Sun Aug 30 2026\n\nScattered showers continue to develop this morning from northern \nIllinois extending east across southern Lake Michigan into much of \ncentral Lower Michigan. This showers are developing along an axis of \nstrengthening low/mid level theta-e advection and coincident\nwith strengthening isentropic upglide. Some respectable \nelevated moisture advection should provide some 100-300 J/kg \nMUCAPEs in a relatively narrow axis from northern Illinois into \ncentral Lower Michigan this morning where moisture transport \nappears to be maximized this morning. A subtle, weak mid level \nshort wave also appears to be kicking out of northeast \nIowa/southeast Minnesota which should further enhance isentropic\nforcing closer to 12Z this morning. Will maintain an isolated \nthunder mention and scattered rain shower PoPs across these \nlocations. Outside of this narrow MUCAPE axis, low level \nmoisture transport is somewhat more limited across the remainder\nof the forecast area, so some question as to southeast extent \nof isolated thunder potential and higher PoPs. Otherwise, warm \nadvection today should promote warmer conditions with highs in \nthe lower to middle 80s. Scattered showers/cloud cover do make \nthis a below normal confidence temperature forecast today for\nnorthwest half of the area where showers should be more\nnumerous.\n\nBy later this afternoon into this evening guidance generally agrees \nthat stronger isentropic lift in the 310-315k layer should become \nfocused across northwest Ohio and approaching the eastern Great \nLakes, with another zone of stronger advective forcing taking shape \nfrom SE MN into southwest WI as a low level jet refocuses. It is \npossible some low PoPs will need to be extended through tonight \nacross northern locations, but given this weakening trend in \nadvective forcing will keep PoPs below mentionable levels for most \nareas. \n\nFor late tonight into Monday, mid level ridge axis will shift across \nthe southern Great Lakes allowing more pronounced mid level WAA and \nwarm 700 mb temps of +10 to +14 deg C to overspread local area.\nIt is possible some isoalted showers/storms could develop at \nthe nose of this stronger mid level WAA but confidence is low \ngiven lack of deeper mid/upper level forcing. Heat concerns \nstart to increase for Monday due to the persistent low level \nwarm/moist advection with peak afternoon heat indices expected \nto reach the mid 90s.\n\nBy late Monday night into Tuesday low level flow is expected to veer \nmore west-southwest as longwave upper ridging gets somewhat \ndampened by larger scale upper low across US/Canadian border. \nThis westerly flow regime will allow low level theta-e ridge to \nthe west to become more firmly entrenched locally and may \nresult in heat indices around 100 degrees both Tuesday and \nWednesday afternoons. An associated cold front with the southern\nCanadian negative height anomaly is expected to drop across the\nsouthern Great Lakes late Wed into Thu. A conservative approach\nto PoPs still seems in order for late Wednesday given stronger \ndynamic forcing should be located well to the north of the local\narea and likely persistence of warm mid level thermal profiles \nand possible moderate surface based CIN. \n\nMedium range guidance still suggests a transition to more \namplification of longwave pattern late this work week into next\nweekend with position of mean upper ridge axis across central \nCONUS. This should prevent additional significant heat concerns \nbut would likely result in episodes of higher PoPs as some \nnorthwest flow waves interact with a potential instability \ngradient across the region.",
